---
import { pageHref } from "../lib/paths.ts";
import ExportSample from "./ExportSample.astro";
---

<section class="export-help" aria-labelledby="export-help-title">
  <h2 id="export-help-title">How to download your TrainHeroic data</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Sign in with your existing TrainHeroic email and password.</li>
    <li>Review the loaded sessions. Use <strong>Change range</strong> to choose dates.</li>
    <li>Select <strong>Only logged</strong> if you want to exclude unlogged sessions.</li>
    <li>Choose CSV, JSON, or Text from <strong>Format</strong>, then select <strong>Download</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    This unofficial tool reads the workout history available to your account. It does not change
    your training. Your browser signs in to TrainHeroic directly. Read the
    <a href={pageHref("/privacy", Astro.url.pathname)}>privacy policy</a> for storage details.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose a workout export format</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>CSV for Excel or Google Sheets</dt>
    <dd>
      One row per exercise set, with prescribed and performed values in separate columns.
      Open the downloaded CSV in Excel, or import it into Google Sheets to filter dates,
      compare lifts, or build your own charts.
    </dd>
    <dt>JSON for scripts and backups</dt>
    <dd>
      A structured list of workouts with blocks, exercises, sets, and unit information.
      Choose JSON when you need the workout hierarchy or want to process the export with code.
    </dd>
    <dt>Text for reading or printing</dt>
    <dd>
      A readable session log. Open the downloaded file in a text editor to print it or save it
      as a PDF. See the <a href={pageHref("/guides/print-workouts", Astro.url.pathname)}>printing guide</a>.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <ExportSample />

  <details>
    <summary>Which columns are in the TrainHeroic CSV export?</summary>
    <p>The CSV contains these 14 columns, in this order:</p>
    <pre><code>date,program,team,workout,block,exercise,set,prescribed_reps,prescribed_weight,performed_reps,performed_weight,weight_unit,prescribed,performed</code></pre>
    <p>
      The first seven columns identify the session, exercise, and set. The next five contain
      numeric reps and weights where available, plus the weight unit. The final two preserve
      the readable prescription and result, including values such as time or distance that
      do not fit the reps and weight columns. Missing values stay blank. Check
      <code>weight_unit</code> before comparing loads; this export does not convert units.
    </p>
    <p>
      CSV includes exercises with set rows. JSON also retains the session and block structure,
      so use JSON when sessions without sets matter to your analysis.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Can I export an entire program or move it to another training app?</summary>
    <p>
      This tool exports the sessions accessible through your athlete account for the loaded date
      range. It does not download a reusable coach program template or automatically import data
      into another app. Use CSV or JSON as the source for a migration, and check the destination
      app's import format. For programming from a spreadsheet, read the
      <a href={pageHref("/guides/import-workouts", Astro.url.pathname)}>workout import guide</a>.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Is this a full account or GDPR data export?</summary>
    <p>
      This is a workout-history export. It does not include every category of account data, such
      as messages, billing records, or profile information. TrainHeroic offers an
      <a href="https://account.trainheroic.com/data-export">official account-data export</a>.
      Its <a href="https://support.trainheroic.com/hc/en-us/articles/19026188372621-As-an-athlete-how-do-I-download-my-personal-data-GDPR-Export">export instructions</a>
      describe a ZIP of CSV files from the athlete web portal.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Why are sessions or performed values missing?</summary>
    <p>
      Check the date range and the <strong>Only logged</strong> filter first. A prescribed set
      can have blank performed values when no result was recorded. The tool can only export
      data TrainHeroic returns for the signed-in account. If a session you can see in TrainHeroic
      is missing here, include its date and the error text in a
      <a href={pageHref("/support", Astro.url.pathname)}>support request</a>.
    </p>
  </details>
  <p>
    For repeatable downloads and local files, use the
    <a href={pageHref("/developers/cli", Astro.url.pathname)}>TrainHeroic CLI</a>.
    For AI-assisted analysis, <a href={pageHref("/connect", Astro.url.pathname)}>connect TrainHeroic to Claude</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .export-help {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  h2 { margin-block: 1.5rem 0.75rem; font-size: 1.25rem; }
  ol { padding-left: 1.5rem; }
  li + li { margin-top: 0.375rem; }
  dt { margin-top: 1rem; font-weight: 650; }
  dd { margin: 0.25rem 0 1rem; }
  details { padding-block: 0.75rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line); }
  summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; }
  summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent); outline-offset: 4px; }
  details p { margin-top: 0.75rem; }
  pre { overflow-x: auto; padding: 0.75rem; background: var(--color-surface); }
</style>
